Use
\[
D=\epsilon E=\kappa\epsilon_0E
\]
so
\[
E=\frac{D}{\kappa\epsilon_0}
=\frac{2.0\times10^{-6}}{(5.0)(8.85\times10^{-12})}
=4.5\times10^4\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}
\]

Use
\[
D=\kappa\epsilon_0E
\]
so
\[
E=\frac{D}{\kappa\epsilon_0}
=\frac{5.0\times10^{-6}}{(4.0)(8.85\times10^{-12})}
=1.41\times10^5\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}
\]

The free surface charge density equals the jump in normal displacement:
\[
\sigma_f=D_{2n}-D_{1n}
\]
Thus
\[
\sigma_f=(8.0-3.0)\times10^{-6}
=5.0\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{C\,m^{-2}}
\]
with sign defined for the chosen normal direction.

At a charge-free dielectric boundary,
\[
D_{1n}=D_{2n}
\]
so
\[
\kappa_1\epsilon_0E_{1n}=\kappa_2\epsilon_0E_{2n}
\]
which gives
\[
\frac{E_{2n}}{E_{1n}}=\frac{\kappa_1}{\kappa_2}
\]
The tangential electric field is continuous:
\[
E_{1t}=E_{2t}
\]
If \(\theta\) is measured from the normal, then
\[
\tan\theta=\frac{E_t}{E_n}
\]
Therefore
\[
\frac{\tan\theta_2}{\tan\theta_1}
=\frac{E_{2t}/E_{2n}}{E_{1t}/E_{1n}}
=\frac{E_{1n}}{E_{2n}}
=\frac{\kappa_2}{\kappa_1}
\]
